 "title": "Using the Fake Name Method for Social Media Content",
 "topic": "content creation",
 "summary": "The creator explains the 'Fake Name' method, which involves giving catchy made-up titles to observed real-world processes to generate more social media views. By applying memorable names to real phenomena, creators can make their content feel more legitimate and relatable. The creator argues this practice expands language rather than misleads viewers.",
 "key_points": [
  "The 'Fake Name' method means placing a made-up name in front of an observed process to make it seem legitimate and gain social media views.",
  "Example 1: The creator coined 'The Fake Choice Effect' (1.1M views) to describe how companies make people feel in control of trade-offs when they are not.",
  "Example 2: The creator called a black credit card 'The Batman Card' (821.6K views) to create familiarity, even though that is not its official name.",
  "The creator counters claims that this practice gaslights viewers, arguing it adds useful terminology to language just like phrases such as 'carrot on a stick'.",
  "Language evolves through everyday people inventing words to describe real-world phenomena rather than top-down declarations."
 ],
 "content": "Post Text:\nThe “Fake Name” Method #money\n\nMake a note of this post.\n\nTranscript:\nThe fake naming method is when you put a made-up name in front of a process that you observe, just to make it seem more legitimate so you could farm more views on social media. And when you perform this method correctly, it gets you millions of views without even trying. So how does it work? Well, let's look at an example. The Fake Choice Effect is when companies make you think that you're in control of the tradeoffs, even if you're really not. Now that's a perfect example of the fake naming effect. I saw an effect that was cool, but there was no established name for it, so I just made up the name to make it seem like a legitimate effect that already exists. I did the exact same thing here. The Batman credit card actually exists, and you won't believe the perks that come with it. The name of this card is not the Batman credit card. I made that up, but it brings familiarity and actually makes it feel way more real. And most people say, well, you shouldn't do this, this is just gaslighting your viewers, but I disagree. By coming up with real-world names for processes that actually exist, like the carrot on a stick effect, you're not taking away from language, you're in fact adding to it. New words aren't made by the president announcing what the new word should be, they're made by everyday people like you and me finding new words to describe stuff that we see in the world. I'm Money Mind and I teach you about money, one fake name at a time.\n\nText Shown On Screen:\n- The “Fake Name” Method\n- The “Batman” Card (821.6K views)\n- The Fake Choice Effect (1.1M views)\n- 1.6M views video\n- The Patel Method (911.6K views)",
